course: everyday
lesson: 341
title: Cheating in games and sports
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Games only work if everyone plays by the rules. Ben in Austin pressed the reset button every time he was losing a video game with his friend Luis. One day Luis said, «You always do that», and went home. For two weeks Luis didn't want to play with him. Ben said sorry, and now they play to the end, even when Ben loses. Losing well keeps your friends.
explain: Not every cheat is seen. Ben pretended to have a sore leg so he wouldn't come last on sports day. Nobody guessed. He sat and watched, bored and guilty, while his team ran one runner short. That evening he told his teacher the truth, and in the next race he ran — and came fifth, not last, and felt proud, and his team trusted him again.
explain: Some cheats are quiet: a timer that ran for five minutes written down as twenty, a score changed on the class quiz, numbers skipped in hide and seek. They are usually found out by small things — a different pen colour, not knowing the story, everyone still running when you open your eyes.
explain: Winning by cheating is not really winning: the others stop the game, stop trusting you, and you have not got any better at anything.
fact: Games only work and stay fun when everyone follows the same rules.
fact: Cheating to win usually ends the game, because others stop wanting to play.
fact: Losing well, by finishing the game and shaking hands, keeps friends.
fact: Faking an injury to avoid a race lets your team down and leaves you feeling guilty.
fact: Coming last after trying is better than not trying at all.
fact: Writing down reading time you didn't do doesn't make you read.
fact: Changing a score doesn't change what you know.
fact: Peeking under the blindfold in blind man's buff is soon seen, and then the others won't let you be «it» any more.
fact: Times tables you learn stay with you when there is no calculator.
fact: Getting a friend to whisper answers can cost the whole team points.
fact: The fix for cheating in a game is to say sorry and play again fairly.
exercise: Why did Ben press reset when he was losing? | He was angry and wanted to win | The game had frozen on the screen | Luis asked him to press it for him | He wanted to start a new level instead
exercise: What happened after Luis said «You always do that»? | Luis went home and didn't want to play again | Luis laughed it off and kept on playing happily | Luis pressed reset too and they started again | Nothing happened, because Luis didn't care at all
exercise: How did Ben win Luis back? | He said sorry and played to the end, even when he lost | He let Luis win every single time from then on, so Luis was happy | He gave Luis the controller forever | He found a new friend
exercise: A girl looks under her blindfold in blind man's buff. What usually happens? | The others see and say she can't be «it» any more | She wins the game quickly and everyone cheers for her | Nobody ever notices | The game becomes more fun
exercise: A boy writes «20 minutes» in his reading record but the timer ran for five. How is he found out? | He can't answer questions about the story | The timer sends a message to the school about it | His book is still new | His mum counts the pages
exercise: A boy changes his score on the class quiz with a different pen. Why doesn't it help him? | It doesn't change what he knows, and the colour gives it away | It makes him top of the class forever and nobody can change it | Teachers never check scores | Quizzes don't matter
exercise: A girl counts fast and skips numbers in hide and seek. What happens? | The others are still running, call her a cheat and stop | Everyone is found much faster and the whole game is happier | Nobody notices fast counting | The game becomes fair
exercise: A boy uses a calculator in his times tables test and the teacher hears the beeps. What is the right way to put it right? | Say sorry and practise the tables every night | Turn the sound off on the calculator next time | Say the calculator was his friend's | Ask for an easier test
exercise: Why is learning the sevens better than using a calculator? | The tables stay in your head when the calculator is gone | Calculators are always wrong with the hard tables like the sevens | Teachers hate calculators | The sevens are not on calculators
exercise: In a quiz a boy gets his friend to whisper answers. The quizmaster hears. What happens to the team? | It loses a point, and maybe the quiz | It wins a bonus point for good teamwork | Nothing at all happens to the team then | The friend's team gets the point instead
exercise: Ben faked a sore leg on sports day and nobody guessed. How did he feel? | Bored and guilty, watching his team run one short | Happy and proud that he had missed the hard race | Proud of his clever trick | Nothing, as nobody knew
exercise: Why is coming last better than faking an injury? | You tried, you were honest and your team had its runner | Nobody watches the last runner, so it doesn't matter at all | Last place gets a prize | Faking is not allowed on Mondays
exercise: What did Ben do to put the sore-leg fib right? | Told the truth and ran the next race | Asked his team to forget all about it and say nothing | Faked a sore arm in the next race instead | Said nothing and hoped for rain on race day
exercise: When you are losing a game with a friend, what is the wise choice? | Finish the game and say «well played» | Press reset quickly before it ends | Say the game is broken | Stop talking to your friend
exercise: Which is TRUE about winning by cheating? | It isn't really winning, and others stop trusting you | It is still a real win as long as nobody ever finds out | It is fine in video games | Nobody minds if you win
test: Luis on the phone: «not coming round, u always reset when ur losing». What would win Luis back? | Say sorry and promise to play every game to the end | Say Luis is a sore loser and he resets too | Buy a new game | Let Luis reset too
test: «I always skip numbers in hide n seek, they never notice». What is likely? | They notice, call it cheating and stop playing | They think it's really clever and start to copy it | They never notice | They count faster too
test: Teacher, cold: «Why is your quiz score in blue when everyone used black?» What's the honest answer? | «I changed it. Sorry, I'd rather be in the middle honestly» | «You must have written it in blue yourself, miss, it was like that» | «My pen ran out» | «Someone else changed it»
test: Mum, reading the record: «20 mins? You can't even tell me who's in the story.» What should the boy do? | Own up and set the timer properly from now on | Make up the story's names quickly and hope | Say the story had no people in it | Say the timer was broken
test: Kid in the yard: «I faked sick on sports day last year and the year before, worked both times, so it's fine». What is true? | It still lets the team down and leaves him not trying | It's fine since it worked two years running and nobody got hurt | It's fine if you're slow | It only matters on big days
test: Teammate, annoyed: «we lost by ONE point cos you got whispered answers». What is the best reply? | «You're right, sorry, team» | «It wasn't my fault at all, honest» | «The quizmaster was mean to us» | «You should've whispered louder»
test: Teacher: «I can hear beeping, Callum.» What is the right next step for Callum? | Put the calculator away, own up and practise | Say it's his watch going off | Hide it in his sleeve | Say everyone has one
test: Sister after blind man's buff: «you're peeking, I saw your head tip back!» What helps most? | Say sorry and play with the blindfold properly | Peek less obviously next time, just a tiny gap | Say she's a liar | Stop the game
test: Which is the best thing to say when you lose a game you badly wanted to win? | «Well played, can we have another go?» | «You cheated, I saw you!» | «That doesn't count» | «This game's rubbish»
test: Ben lost his race but ran it honestly. What did he gain? | His team's trust and his own pride | A gold medal for trying so hard | Nothing at all | A day off school
test: «just say ur leg hurts, you'll come last anyway». What is the wise answer? | Run anyway; last after trying beats faking | Okay, a sore leg is a good excuse | Say your arm hurts instead | Ask Mum for a note
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the resetting of a game by a participant who is losing. | It constitutes cheating and commonly causes other participants to cease playing | It is permissible where the game is electronic, since no physical object is affected | It constitutes a legitimate tactic | It is of no consequence to other participants
exam: Identify the principal consequence of the feigning of an injury in order to avoid participation in a race on behalf of a team. | The team is deprived of a runner and the pupil acts dishonestly | The team gains an advantage, since a slower runner is thereby replaced by another | The pupil is thereby rendered a faster runner | No consequence of any description ensues
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the alteration of a score subsequent to its recording? | It alters the record but not the knowledge of the pupil | It improves the knowledge of the pupil, since the higher mark encourages him | It is permissible where unobserved | It is required by the rules of the quiz
exam: Determine the conduct most conducive to the continuance of friendships when a game is lost. | The completion of the game and the congratulation of the winner | The interruption of the game prior to its conclusion | The accusation of the winner of dishonesty | The refusal to play on any future occasion
